    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/588323/senior-integration-developer": {
      "title": "Senior Integration Developer",
      "description": "Senior Integration Developer Apply now Job no: 588323 Work type: Full-time Location: Flexible Categories: Information Technology, Technical Based at either Melbourne or Geelong Campuses + hybrid work arrangements Full-time and continuing HEW 8 $123,554 - $141,459 + 17% Superannuation Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job - we offer a lifestyle. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne, and regional Victoria, you’ll find everything you need to thrive: on-site childcare, gyms, medical centres, cafes, accessible public transport, and convenient parking. Enjoy flexible working arrangements that support your work-life balance, generous leave entitlements including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional time off, plus a wide range of staff discounts on travel and more. Our health and wellbeing services, ongoing professional development opportunities, and inclusive, supportive culture make Deakin a place where you can grow - whether you're working remotely or immersed in the vibrant energy of campus life. About the role and team: Join Deakin’s Integration Development team within Infrastructure and Digital, responsible for building and supporting the integrations that connect enterprise applications, cloud platforms and digital services across the University. Reporting to the Group Manager, Integration Development, this is a hands-on senior technical role working closely with architects, analysts, delivery teams and business stakeholders. As a Senior Integration Developer, you will: Lead the end-to-end delivery of complex MuleSoft integration solutions, from design and development through testing, deployment and support Design secure, reliable and scalable APIs and integrations using contemporary integration patterns and technologies Provide technical leadership through design and code reviews, technical guidance and collaboration with stakeholders across the University Drive improvements in DevOps, automated testing, code quality, reuse and operational reliability To be successful, you’ll have: Postgraduate qualifications or extensive relevant experience and management expertise; or equivalent combination of experience and/or education/training Significant experience designing, developing and supporting complex integration solutions in a large enterprise and cloud technology environment Strong hands-on experience developing and supporting solutions using the MuleSoft Anypoint Platform Strong experience designing REST and SOAP APIs and integration solutions using appropriate patterns, including event-driven architectures and messaging Strong experience across the software development lifecycle (SDLC) and with contemporary DevOps practices, including source control, continuous integration, automated testing and code-quality controls Demonstrated technical leadership and stakeholder-engagement experience, including working with or guiding technical teams in an Agile delivery environment Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Darren Blizzard (Group Manager, Integration Development) on darren.blizzard@deakin.edu.au . For a copy of the position description, please see below: Senior Integration Developer - HEW 8_PD.pdf Applications for this position close on Sunday 1st November. Make an Impact with Us Together we aim to take Deakin forward, strengthening our progressive University through the digital work we do. Whether it's empowering students, enabling teaching staff, or supporting researchers, the work you do here matters. Your achievements contribute to making a positive difference in the lives of the people and communities we serve. So that you can focus on making a positive impact, Deakin Digital Services lays the foundations for you to be your best self, grow and evolve and join forces with exceptional people. Be Your Best Self As a unique individual we understand that showing up as the best version of yourself takes commitment to your personal wellbeing. That’s why we foster a supportive environment where you can manage your work to balance your productivity, engagement and wellbeing. Our approach to flexible working empowers you to achieve your best. Grow & Evolve Being in the higher education sector, ours is an environment of knowledge sharing and seeking. With us you can grow your capability through formal and informal learning and continuously improve, deepen and broaden your expertise. With many digital roles and teams under the one umbrella, there’s opportunity to expand your skillset, embrace new challenges and evolve your career. When YOU thrive, WE thrive. Join Forces As a member of our dynamic team, you’ll tackle challenges and deliver digital services and solutions alongside supportive, inclusive and talented humans from within the team, and more broadly across the University. We are a powerful collective of diverse skillsets, strengths and experience and our practices promote an environment of collaboration where your unique contribution is welcomed and valued. When you join forces with Digital Services you join forces with Deakin. Visit Why Work at Deakin to view the overarching benefits of working with Deakin. Are you ready to make an impact with us? We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 16 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589244/lecturer-in-civil-engineering-educationfocused": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Civil Engineering, Education-Focused",
      "description": "Lecturer in Civil Engineering, Education-Focused Apply now Job no: 589244 Work type: Education-focused Academic, Full-time Location: Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Engineering Based at our Melbourne, Burwood Campus 2 Full-time and continuing opportunities in the School of Engineering Level B $122,391 - $144,759 + 17% Superannuation Applications are currently being accepted exclusively from current and former sessional academics at Deakin. Applicants must have been engaged as a sessional academic at Deakin University within two years of the advertising date to be eligible. Applications that do not meet this criterion will not be considered at this time. Make an Impact The School of Engineering is seeking two education-focused academics to join its growing civil engineering team at the Melbourne Burwood Campus. These continuing appointments will strengthen teaching capacity as the school expands its Burwood engineering offering and prepares for further growth in 2027 and beyond. We are recruiting one Lecturer with expertise in Structural Engineering and one Lecturer with expertise in Infrastructure and Transport Engineering. Both roles will contribute to high-quality, project-based engineering education and help students connect technical knowledge with contemporary professional practice. Deakin’s Education-focused Academic roles offer a meaningful career pathway for those whose strengths lie in teaching and learning. These roles are tailored for academics dedicated to student-centred teaching, teaching excellence and innovative educational practices. We invite applications from current and former sessional academics engaged with Deakin within the past two years. What Does This Role Offer These roles are suited to people with an emerging record and passion for learning and teaching, and a keen interest in contributing to community engagement for teaching and employability. Based at the Burwood Campus, these roles will support the School’s civil engineering curriculum and make a significant contribution to teaching excellence, curriculum development and student success. One Lecturer will bring expertise in structural engineering, including areas such as structural analysis and design, and construction materials. The other will bring expertise in infrastructure and transport engineering, including areas such as transport planning, traffic systems and sustainable and resilient infrastructure. Both will work collaboratively to design and deliver inclusive, engaging and authentic learning experiences across campus, online and blended modes. To be successful, you’ll meet the following Key Selection Criteria: PhD in Civil Engineering and/or other relevant qualifications and experience Excellent record of scholarly learning and teaching in UG and/or PG programs, including innovative curriculum design Demonstrated passion for teaching and learning Ability to deliver effective teaching practices to improve student success and engagement Emerging reputation in research and scholarship through publications and/or exhibitions and/or success in obtaining external research funding. Ability to contribute to communities through research. Capacity to contribute to leadership of research and administration. Excellent interpersonal skills and a proven ability to establish good working relationships with colleagues. Demonstrated passion for teaching and learning. Ability to deliver effective teaching practices to improve student success and engagement. Here's how to apply: To be eligible to apply, you must currently be a sessional academic at Deakin or have been previously engaged as a sessional academic at Deakin within the last two years of the advertising date. To find out if you are eligible to apply, please contact Deakin Help on +61 3 5247 9999 or peopleconnect@deakin.edu.au Please submit your updated resume, a short cover letter and a separate document addressing the Key Selection Criteria. Please indicate in your cover letter which Lecturer role you are applying for. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Emily Heap, School Manager, School of Engineering via e.heap@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: EFA Level B_Civil Engineering.pdf Applications for this position close on 30 October 2026 at 11:55pm What We Offer Whether you're looking to develop new skills, gain exposure to a different area, or take the next step in your career journey, this opportunity offers a chance to broaden your experience and continue your career development within our thriving and accomplished university. Here at Deakin, we continue to encourage our people to explore new opportunities, build impactful connections across Deakin, and contribute to meaningful work that continues to support our students, staff, and communities. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 16 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589977/strategic-adviser": {
      "title": "Strategic Adviser",
      "description": "Strategic Adviser Apply now Job no: 589977 Work type: Full-time Location: Flexible, Geelong - City, Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Management, Administration Based at either our Melbourne Burwood or Geelong Waterfront Campus with hybrid work available Fulltime and fixed-term for 5 years Attractive remuneration package and benefits including 17% Superannuation Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job - we offer a lifestyle. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne, and regional Victoria, you’ll find everything you need to thrive: on-site childcare, gyms, medical centres, cafes, accessible public transport, and convenient parking. Enjoy flexible working arrangements that support your work-life balance, generous leave entitlements including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional time off, plus a wide range of staff discounts on travel and more. Our health and wellbeing services, ongoing professional development opportunities, and inclusive, supportive culture make Deakin a place where you can grow - whether you're working remotely or immersed in the vibrant energy of campus life. Context of this position The Strategic Adviser is a pivotal leadership role, partnering directly with the Deputy Vice-Chancellor University Services to shape strategic direction, drive university-wide priorities and deliver meaningful organisational impact. Drawing on deep specialist expertise, strategic insight and exceptional judgement, the role transforms complex challenges and emerging opportunities into innovative, practical solutions that inform executive decision-making and advance significant programs of work. Operating with a high degree of autonomy, the Strategic Adviser connects people, ideas and expertise across organisational boundaries, influencing senior stakeholders, strengthening collaboration and aligning activity to achieve shared outcomes. Through thought leadership, trusted advice and a commitment to continuous improvement, the role strengthens professional practice, builds capability and helps position the University to respond confidently to evolving priorities and the changing higher education landscape. As the Strategic Advisor, you will: Strategic Leadership and Delivery: Lead a defined portfolio or specialist function, translating University priorities into practical strategies, frameworks and deliverables while adapting to evolving needs and ensuring high-quality outcomes. Strategic Advice and Decision-Making : Provide expert advice to senior leaders on complex operational and strategic matters, using sound judgement, critical analysis and evidence-based recommendations to influence decisions and direction. Continuous Improvement and Quality Assurance : Drive the development and improvement of systems, processes, frameworks and professional practices to enhance quality, consistency, efficiency and responsiveness to stakeholder needs. People Leadership and Capability Development: Lead, support and develop team members by setting clear expectations, fostering accountability, providing constructive feedback and promoting continuous learning and high performance. Stakeholder Engagement and Collaboration : Build productive relationships across the University and with external partners, collaborating to align priorities, resolve complex issues and achieve shared strategic objectives. Strategic Alignment and Organisational Impact: Apply specialist expertise, interpret policies and emerging trends, and contribute to broader strategic initiatives, demonstrating autonomy, initiative and flexibility to deliver sustainable outcomes in a complex and evolving environment. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Briony Lewis (Interim Deputy Vice-Chancellor, University Services) on briony.lewis@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: Senior Leader 1 - Strategic Adviser, University Services (2).pdf Applications for this position close on Thursday 29 th October, 11:55pm. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 16 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589821/campus-life-officer": {
      "title": "Campus Life Officer",
      "description": "Campus Life Officer Apply now Job no: 589821 Work type: Full-time Location: Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Student Services Based at our Burwood Campus Full time and continuing $86,744 - $97,290 (HEW 5) + 17% Superannuation Why this role matters Ready to create experiences that make a real difference? Join Deakin Residential Services as a Campus Life Officer and play an important role in shaping residential life for students. Working closely with the Campus Life Coordinator, you'll help deliver engaging programs, events and initiatives that foster connection, belonging and a positive community culture across our residences. From supporting student leaders and coordinating activities to promoting wellbeing and helping students navigate university life, you'll contribute to creating an environment where students feel supported, connected and empowered to succeed. We're looking for an energetic, approachable and empathetic individual who thrives in a student-focused environment and is passionate about building vibrant communities. As this role is centred on supporting students where they live, a strong on-campus presence is essential. The position operates on a fortnightly rotating roster: Week 1: Tuesday to Saturday, 8.00am to 4.00pm Week 2: Monday to Friday, 11.00am to 7.00pm The role also requires evening, weekend and out-of-hours work to support residential programs, events and student needs. If you're motivated by creating positive student experiences, building community and helping students thrive, this is your opportunity to make a genuine impact every day. As a Campus Life Officer, you will: Support student wellbeing and uphold community standards, helping to create a safe, inclusive and respectful living environment. Design and deliver engaging events, programs and campaigns that foster connection, belonging and an exceptional residential student experience. Support the recruitment, training and development of Residential Leaders, empowering students to make a positive impact within their community. Provide guidance, coaching and support to Residential Leaders, contributing to the delivery of successful programs and positive community outcomes. Build strong relationships with students, university stakeholders and community partners to enhance engagement and strengthen the residential experience. To be successful, you’ll have: A degree and relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education/training. Experience coordinating and delivering programs, events or initiatives that foster engagement, participation and positive community outcomes. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build positive relationships and engage effectively with students from diverse backgrounds. Experience providing guidance, support and mentoring to student leaders, volunteers or peer-based teams. A genuine passion for student wellbeing, community building and creating inclusive environments where students can thrive. This position is designated a flexible ordinary hours span position due to operational needs. A 20% flexible loading applies to every hour worked outside the University Ordinary Hours of Work. Information about Flexible Ordinary Hours Span can be found in the Deakin Enterprise Agreement under Clause 43. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume, your position preference, and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Itai Avisar (Campus Life Coordinator) on i.avisar@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD - Campus Life Officer, 589821.pdf Applications for this position close on Wednesday 28th October at 11:55pm. Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job. We offer a rewarding career in a flexible, inclusive and forward-thinking environment. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne and regional Victoria, you'll enjoy flexible working arrangements that support work-life balance, generous leave provisions including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional leave, and access to a range of professional development opportunities to help you grow your career. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 15 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
